17592186045417] [17592186414916 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186414876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414916 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414916 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414916 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186414840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414916 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f6500bd7-63b7-5ff0-8a52-5b1a962545e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414917 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414917 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125235" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414917 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414917 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate ${∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=-x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414917 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "019e6ad0-f5c1-533f-a132-64a664a1964d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414917 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-8+8 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414918 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414918 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414918 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414918 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin traversed counterclockwise from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(-3,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414918 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17a6c1da-e7e7-5509-a937-e43376741415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414918 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin. This curve can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+9{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+9{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+27{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(3{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(3{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(3{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & =9{[{sin}^{3}⁡t-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{2}]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =9[0-(1-\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414919 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414919 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125055" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414919 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414919 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=4(x+y)\\,i-3x\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(2,1)$ to the point $B(3,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414919 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "32efcb37-d58e-5a20-a421-a7a716960b49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414919 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=2\\,i+j$ and $b=3\\,i+3\\,j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(2\\,i+j)+t[(3\\,i+3\\,j)-(2\\,i+j)] \\\\  & =(2\\,i+j)+t(i+2j) \\\\  & =(t+2)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t+2\\,y=2t+1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =4(x+y)\\,i-3x\\,j \\\\  & =4(3t+3)\\,i-(3t+6)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)j \\\\  & =i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(4(3t+3)\\,i-(3t+6)\\,j)\\cdot (i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =4(3t+3)\\cdot 1-(3t+6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6t\\,dt \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414920 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414920 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414920 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414920 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(y-x)^{2}\\,i-xy\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(-1,-1)$ to the point $B(-2,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414920 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "421f01b5-2cf6-557c-be0b-ca7da3ee40a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414920 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=-i-j$ and $b=-2\\,i.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i-j)+t[-2\\,i-(-i-j)] \\\\  & =(-i-j)+t(-i+j) \\\\  & =(-1-t)\\,i+(t-1)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=-1-t,\\,y=t-1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y-x)^{2}\\,i-xy\\,j \\\\  & =(t-1+t+1)^{2}\\,i-(t-1)(-1-t)\\,j \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)j \\\\  & =-i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(4{t}^{2}\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3{t}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-3{t}^{2}-1\\,dt \\\\  & =(-{t}^{3}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-1-1)-(-0-0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414921 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414921 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125042" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414921 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414921 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,i+{y}^{6}\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(-1,1)$ to the point $B(1,2)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(-1+2t)\\,i+(1+t)\\,j.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414921 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "539feec3-1822-501c-b96b-4b9de8ad853a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414921 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=-\\,i+\\,j$ and $b=i+2\\,j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+\\,j)+t[(i+2\\,j)-(-i+j)] \\\\  & =(-i+\\,j)+t(2\\,i+\\,j) \\\\  & =(-1+2t)\\,i+(1+t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=-1+2t,\\,y=1+t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,i+{y}^{6}\\,j \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{-1+2t}\\,i+(1+t)^{6}\\,j \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}\\,i+(t+1)^{6}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1+2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}\\,i+(t+1)^{6}\\,j)\\cdot (2\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}\\cdot 2+(t+1)^{6}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}+(t+1)^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}+(t+1)^{6}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414922 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414922 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125044" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414922 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414922 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{x-y}\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(-2,1)$ to the point $B(1,-1)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(-2+3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414922 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6205682e-6a34-5394-bdf6-22819f2b4d42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414922 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=-2\\,i+j$ and $b=i-j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(-2\\,i+j)+t[(i-j)-(-2\\,i+j)] \\\\  & =(-2\\,i+j)+t(3\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(-2+3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=-2+3t,\\,y=1-2t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x+y)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{x-y}\\,j \\\\  & =(-2+3t+1-2t)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{-2+3t-(1-2t)}\\,j \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{5t-3}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2+3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i-2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((t-1)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{5t-2}\\,j)\\cdot (3\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\cdot 3+{e}^{5t-3}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =3(t-1)^{2}-2{e}^{5t-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3(t-1)^{2}-2{e}^{5t-3}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414923 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414923 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125143" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414923 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414923 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\sqrt{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=x-2$ traversed from the point $A(2,1)$ to the point $B(4,{e}^{2})?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2)\\,i+t\\,j.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414923 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7481ae6d-9732-54c0-9a0f-c47726a9f80a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414923 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. This is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2)\\,i+t\\,j,\\,1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}\\,i+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}\\,i+t{e}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\sqrt{t}\\,i+t{e}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+t{e}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}+{e}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}+{e}^{2}t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414924 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414924 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414924 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414924 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}$ traversed from the point $A(0,3)$ to the point $B(4,5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414924 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78546b3f-b852-5bf0-8988-10eb32837ae5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414924 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Since $y$ is the subject of the equation, a parametrization is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =t\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9})j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}\\,j)\\cdot (i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\,j) \\\\  & =t\\cdot 1+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}} \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414925 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414925 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125036" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414925 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414925 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y={e}^{x}$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(2,{e}^{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414925 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "88b1af01-ca8c-54ff-810f-7581fa6aee53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414925 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Since $y$ is the subject of the equation, a parametrization is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{t}\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot (i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}+t{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({e}^{t}+t{e}^{t})\\,dt \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414926 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414926 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76984" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414926 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414926 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fb/fb8c2591ed5068812fa1d184d4a93a6c71b0d2ef77bb01debd4b2a03211742f7.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=2x\\,i+5y\\,j$ and $C$ is the the arc of the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $2$ and $4$ respectively, traversed anticlockwise from $A(2,0)$ to $B(0,4).$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414926 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d9322f25-3d78-5fb4-87e7-1e944f82fbf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414926 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the parametrization of an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $2$ and $4$ respectively. It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2x\\,i+5y\\,j \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+20\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+20\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+20\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =72\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414927 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414927 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414927 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414927 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y={e}^{2x}$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,{e}^{2})?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=t\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\,j.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414927 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e11373c8-2017-5040-89cd-e12dc0c5ae7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414927 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. This is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2t}\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})j \\\\  & =i+2{e}^{2t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({t}^{2}\\,i+2t\\,j)\\cdot (i+2{e}^{2t}\\,j) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot 1+2t\\cdot 2{e}^{2t} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4t{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}+4t{e}^{2t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t(t+4{e}^{2t})\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414928 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414928 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125045" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414928 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414928 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(-1,-2)$ to the point $B(3,4)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(-1+4t)\\,i+(-2+6t)\\,j.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414928 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f6d2f16c-f6f5-5918-ae9d-9d7f59f7eba9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414928 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=-i-2\\,j$ and $b=3\\,i+4\\,j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i-2\\,j)+t[(3\\,i+4\\,j)-(-i-2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i-2\\,j)+t(4\\,i+6\\,j) \\\\  & =(-1+4t)\\,i+(-2+6t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=-1+4t,\\,y=-2+6t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-1+4t+2-6t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1+4t-2+6t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t-3)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1+4t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2+6t)j \\\\  & =4\\,i+6\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t-3)\\,j)\\cdot (4\\,i+6\\,j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-2t)\\cdot 4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t-3)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-2t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-2t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t-3)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
